## PHY-003 — two-/three-cell composition

PASS_CONDITIONAL_PM. The declared retained-mean coupling satisfies zero-coupling recovery, D12 covariance, finite normalization, explicit pair/triple composition and nontrivial correlations. At N=2, g=3.7, kappa=0.25 the pair MI is `0.0176458` nats and the three-cell endpoints acquire MI `9.44157e-05` nats through the middle cell.

But a preregistered full-p coupling has the same isolated-cell core and different coupled predictions. Their pair distributions differ by TV `0.0454045`. Thus the composition law is **not sourced by MP7**.

## PHY-004 — informational continuity

MIXED. Universal preservation is refuted: exact states `p_+ != p_-` have identical `X7^T p=0` and TV distance 1/3. Any decoder from that retained mean alone incurs at least TV 1/6 on one witness. The missing tangent subspace is 4D (Fourier modes k=1,2).

A strong restricted positive theorem survives: on the minimal Gibbs exponential family `p(theta)=softmax(X theta)`, strict convexity makes `theta -> mu` injective, so `mu` exactly recovers `p` on that family. This is family-relative sufficiency, not universal information conservation and not unitarity.

## PHY-005 — event causality

PASS_CONDITIONAL_PM. Metropolis and Barker rules were constructed for the same equilibrium law and independently satisfy detailed balance, proving equilibrium does not select unique kinetics. Raw state reachability is useless as causality because the finite chain is irreducible (one SCC).

Supported events do give a nontrivial order structure. Nonadjacent updates commute exactly; adjacent ones do not. A local intervention can influence a neighbor after one event, cannot influence distance two after one event, and can after two ordered events. For Metropolis the finite scout found max TV `0.0919424` at distance two after two events.

## PHY-006 — propagation

PASS_CONDITIONAL_PM for sparse bounded-degree supports. For discrete event depth, influence is exactly zero outside graph distance `t`. For continuous rate<=1 clocks and maximum degree Delta,

`I_xy(t) <= min(1, sum_{n>=d(x,y)} (Delta t)^n/n!)`.

The bound is uniform in total system size M whenever Delta is uniformly bounded. Dense complete graphs fail the intended locality class because Delta=M-1 and every cell directly supports every other. Long-range supports have immediate tails; `r^-2` weights can retain a bounded weighted influence row sum, whereas unnormalised `r^-1` grows logarithmically.

Crucially, Gamma is still an explicit modelling premise. PHY-006 shows that FIN-compatible local dynamics **can preserve** locality once sparse support is supplied; it does not derive that support.

